  "scope_statement": "Claimed: on 1,332 identical inputs \u2014 the 1,220 the standing row is already measured on, plus poisoned, overflow, edge-valid and real-producer tiers (real Bitget hourly candles, a seven-variant strategy family net of 12 bps) \u2014 ARGUS's current deflated_sharpe returns 0 silent-wrong outputs, against 5 for vectorbt behind a pydantic contract and 57 behind numpy/scipy trapping; and the same derandomized Hypothesis search that found 23 failure classes across ARGUS's gates before adaptation finds 0 now. Where the general-purpose arms still fail \u2014 pydantic's contract passes vectorbt's 0.5 on an overflowing Sharpe and its 1.0 on a single trial; the numpy trap cannot see a NaN that arrives as an argument \u2014 the counts are in the artefact, run, not argued.\n\nNOT claimed: that ARGUS was ahead before this comparison. It was behind the general-purpose contract on the property the capability is named for, and the fix was taken from that contract. Not claimed: that Hypothesis finding nothing proves there is nothing; it is a bounded search. Not claimed: that refusing an overflowing input beats computing it \u2014 a refusal is the acceptable answer the grading allows, not the ideal one. Not claimed: that the numpy trap loses on real data \u2014 wrapped around the whole pipeline rather than the gate alone, it refuses at the source every real case ARGUS refuses at the gate, and ties there. Not claimed: that a gate can detect a caller passing excess kurtosis where Pearson is expected; vectorbt's own accessor does exactly that, and no argument check can tell the two apart.\n"
